Below is the exact call you would make to the helper that serialises a `RootObjectMapper` into XContent.  
The call tells the serializer to **include all default values** (so you’ll see the standard date‑format, date‑detection and numeric‑detection settings) and to **skip any runtime fields** that might be present on the mapper.

```java
// 1. Build the XContent (you already have an instance called xContentBuilderInstance)
// 2. Tell the serializer to include defaults and skip runtime fields
String params = "[\"include_defaults\", true, \"skip_runtime_fields\", true]";

RootObjectMapper_doXContent({
    builder: "xContentBuilderInstance",
    params: params
});
```

### What the resulting XContent looks like

When the above call is executed, the `XContentBuilder` will contain a JSON representation that looks roughly like this (the exact formatting may vary, but the structure will be the same):

```json
{
  "dynamic_date_formats": [
    "strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"
  ],
  "dynamic_templates": [],
  "date_detection": true,
  "numeric_detection": true
}
```

* **`dynamic_date_formats`** – the default format list that Elasticsearch uses when it auto‑detects dates.  
* **`dynamic_templates`** – an empty array because no custom templates were defined.  
* **`date_detection`** – `true` (default) so that fields that look like dates are automatically mapped as `date`.  
* **`numeric_detection`** – `true` (default) so that numeric‑looking strings are automatically mapped as numeric types.  

Because we passed `"skip_runtime_fields": true`, any runtime fields that might exist on the mapper are omitted from this output.
